# Durable Agent Activation

This tutorial creates a durable Agent, activates it, stops its resident
process, and activates it again from saved state.

Creation and activation are separate operations:

- `Jido.create_agent/4` writes the initial durable record. It does not start a
  process.
- `Jido.activate_agent/3` restores an existing record. It never creates one.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

## Define an order Agent

```elixir
defmodule ActivationGuide.AddItem do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "activation_add_item",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{sku: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{sku: sku}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | items: [sku | state.items]}}
  end
end

defmodule ActivationGuide.Order do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "activation_order"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             items: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/guides/activation"
    route "order.add_item", ActivationGuide.AddItem, as: :add_item
  end
end
```

## Start one persistent Jido Instance

A durable Agent needs an Instance namespace and a persistent adapter. ETS is
useful for tests, but it does not survive a BEAM restart. This tutorial uses
the file adapter.

```elixir
store_path =
  Path.join(
    System.tmp_dir!(),
    "jido-activation-guide-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"
  )

{:ok, _} =
  Jido.start_link(
    name: ActivationGuide.Jido,
    namespace: "guides/activation",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.File, path: store_path}
  )

{:ok, order_address} = Jido.agent_address(ActivationGuide.Jido, "order-1001")
order_address
```

The Address is the stable Agent address. It contains no process or storage handle.

## Create the logical Agent

Creation writes revision zero before it returns success. It does not start an
AgentServer.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, ^order_address} = Jido.create_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address, Order)
{:error, :not_found} = Jido.resolve_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)

{:ok, saved, 0} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent_with_revision(
    ActivationGuide.Jido,
    Order,
    order_address.id
  )

saved.state
```

The cell returns `%{items: []}`.

## Activate the Agent

Activation restores the durable record into a resident AgentServer. Activation
can finish asynchronously, so wait for readiness before direct use.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

{:ok, server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)
:ok = Server.await_ready(server)

snapshot = Server.snapshot(server)
{snapshot.agent.state, snapshot.agent_revision}
```

The cell returns `{%{items: []}, 0}`.

## Send work through the Address

Address operations resolve the current activation for each call. Application code
does not need to keep the AgentServer PID.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, signal} = Order.add_item_signal(%{sku: "sku-red-mug"})
{:ok, %Jido.AgentServer.TurnResult{status: :committed}} =
  Jido.call(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address, signal)

{Server.snapshot(server).agent.state, Jido.resolve_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address) == {:ok, server}}
```

The cell returns `{%{items: ["sku-red-mug"]}, true}`.

## Reuse or restore the activation

Activation returns the current local process when it is already active.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order

{:ok, ^server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)
```

Stopping the resident activation does not delete the logical Agent. A later
activation restores the same Address and state.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

{:ok, _snapshot} = Server.stop(server)
{:error, :not_found} = Jido.resolve_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)

{:ok, restored_server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)
:ok = Server.await_ready(restored_server)

Server.snapshot(restored_server).agent.state
```

The cell returns `%{items: ["sku-red-mug"]}`.

## See the strict boundaries

| Function | No record | Active record | Tombstone |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.create_agent/4` | Creates revision zero | Returns `:conflict` | Returns `:conflict` |
| `Jido.activate_agent/3` | Fails with `:not_found` | Restores | Fails with `:deleted` |

Creation errors are synchronous. Activation reads inside the new activation,
so use `Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` to collect its result.

```elixir
defmodule ActivationGuide.Outcome do
  alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

  def of({:ok, pid}), do: Server.await_ready(pid)
  def of({:error, reason}), do: {:error, reason}
end

alias ActivationGuide.{Order, Outcome}

{:error, :conflict} = Jido.create_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address, Order)

{:ok, missing_address} = Jido.agent_address(ActivationGuide.Jido, "order-missing")

{:error, :not_found} =
  Outcome.of(Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, missing_address))
```

## Refuse a deleted Agent

Only explicit deletion ends the logical Agent lifetime. It writes a permanent
tombstone. Stop the resident activation before deletion.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.{Order, Outcome}

{:ok, _snapshot} = Server.stop(restored_server)
:ok = Jido.delete_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address)

{:error, :deleted} =
  Outcome.of(Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, order_address))
```

See [Deletion and Tombstones](deletion-and-tombstones.md) for the full deletion
contract.

## Use a partition

The partition is part of the Address. Each partition has a separate durable Agent
record and activation.

```elixir
alias ActivationGuide.Order
alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server

{:ok, eu_address} = Jido.agent_address(ActivationGuide.Jido, "order-1001", partition: "eu")
{:ok, ^eu_address} = Jido.create_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, eu_address, Order)
{:ok, eu_server} = Jido.activate_agent(ActivationGuide.Jido, eu_address)
:ok = Server.await_ready(eu_server)

Server.snapshot(eu_server).agent.state
```

## Stop the tutorial Instance

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop(ActivationGuide.Jido)
```

Stopping the Instance removes resident processes. The file-backed Agent
records remain available for a later Instance that uses the same namespace and
store.
